> > >What about a new scroll called (for example) "Scroll of
> > >restoration" that when read saves your current game (minus
> > >the scroll, of course) and restores you to it if you die
> > >later on?  This is a bit like save scumming, but would
> > >makes it a legitimate part of the game, and introduce a
> > >whole new branch of strategies for using it.

> > It's just an automatic amulet of lifesaving, nerfed by
> > restoring to a previous fixed point.

> Even randomly, it _might_ be better than lifesaving (For
> instance, being lifesaved in the middle of a horde of
> minotaurs might be less useful than being airlifted back to an
> earlier point)

> More abusively, one can use it to "roll back and retry" all sorts of
> events, e.g use it:
> * before fountain-quaffing, smoky-quaffing, and throne-sitting from
> DL1 on down to get a do-over if no wishes materialize
> * before descending below Medusa, to get a do-over if there's a 1:0
> Castle wand
> * to increase the odds of a particular Medusa/Sokoban level (to go for
> reflection or whatever)

Those last two open up an interesting aspect of the idea: using
it most effectively depends on some knowledge of Nethack
internals.  For example, without looking, I'd guess that the
wand is generated when the castle is generated, and it's charges
are fixed then.  So reading the scroll after you've stepped into
the castle won't change it.  Same thing for the goodies at
Medusa, or the Sokoban level: you have to use it before seeing
the level.  (And quite possibly, some things are fixed even
earlier in the game.)

